[1mCONFIGURATION[0m[m
       The following configuration variables in /etc/login.defs change the behavior of this tool:[m
[m
       [1mLASTLOG_UID_MAX [22m(number)[m
           Highest user ID number for which the lastlog entries should be updated. As higher user IDs are usually tracked by remote user identity and authentication services there is no need to create[m
           a huge sparse lastlog file for them.[m
[m
           No [1mLASTLOG_UID_MAX [22moption present in the configuration means that there is no user ID limit for writing lastlog entries.[m
[m
       [1mMAIL_DIR [22m(string)[m
           The mail spool directory. This is needed to manipulate the mailbox when its corresponding user account is modified or deleted. If not specified, a compile-time default is used. The parameter[m
           CREATE_MAIL_SPOOL in /etc/default/useradd determines whether the mail spool should be created.[m
[m
       [1mMAIL_FILE [22m(string)[m
           Defines the location of the users mail spool files relatively to their home directory.[m
[m
       The [1mMAIL_DIR [22mand [1mMAIL_FILE [22mvariables are used by [1museradd[22m, [1musermod[22m, and [1muserdel [22mto create, move, or delete the user's mail spool.[m
[m
       [1mMAX_MEMBERS_PER_GROUP [22m(number)[m
           Maximum members per group entry. When the maximum is reached, a new group entry (line) is started in /etc/group (with the same name, same password, and same GID).[m
[m
           The default value is 0, meaning that there are no limits in the number of members in a group.[m
[m
           This feature (split group) permits to limit the length of lines in the group file. This is useful to make sure that lines for NIS groups are not larger than 1024 characters.[m
[m
           If you need to enforce such limit, you can use 25.[m
[m
           Note: split groups may not be supported by all tools (even in the Shadow toolsuite). You should not use this variable unless you really need it.[m
[m
       [1mSUB_GID_MIN [22m(number), [1mSUB_GID_MAX [22m(number), [1mSUB_GID_COUNT [22m(number)[m
           If /etc/subuid exists, the commands [1museradd [22mand [1mnewusers [22m(unless the user already have subordinate group IDs) allocate [1mSUB_GID_COUNT [22munused group IDs from the range [1mSUB_GID_MIN [22mto[m
[7m Manual page usermod(8) line 148 (press h for help or q to quit)[27m[K[K           [1mSUB_GID_MAX [22mfor each new user.[m
[m
           The default values for [1mSUB_GID_MIN[22m, [1mSUB_GID_MAX[22m, [1mSUB_GID_COUNT [22mare respectively 100000, 600100000 and 65536.[m
[m
       [1mSUB_UID_MIN [22m(number), [1mSUB_UID_MAX [22m(number), [1mSUB_UID_COUNT [22m(number)[m
           If /etc/subuid exists, the commands [1museradd [22mand [1mnewusers [22m(unless the user already have subordinate user IDs) allocate [1mSUB_UID_COUNT [22munused user IDs from the range [1mSUB_UID_MIN [22mto [1mSUB_UID_MAX[0m[m
           for each new user.[m
[m
           The default values for [1mSUB_UID_MIN[22m, [1mSUB_UID_MAX[22m, [1mSUB_UID_COUNT [22mare respectively 100000, 600100000 and 65536.[m
