[1mDESCRIPTION[0m[m
       GNU [1mtar [22mis an archiving program designed to store multiple files in a single file (an [1marchive[22m), and to manipulate such archives.  The archive can be either a regular file or  a  device  (e.g.  a[m
       tape drive, hence the name of the program, which stands for [1mt[22mape [1mar[22mchiver), which can be located either on the local or on a remote machine.[m
[m
   [1mOption styles[0m[m
       Options to GNU [1mtar [22mcan be given in three different styles.  In [1mtraditional style[22m, the first argument is a cluster of option letters and all subsequent arguments supply arguments to those options[m
       that require them.  The arguments are read in the same order as the option letters.  Any command line words that remain after all options have been processed are treated as non-option arguments:[m
       file or archive member names.[m
[m
       For  example,  the [1mc [22moption requires creating the archive, the [1mv [22moption requests the verbose operation, and the [1mf [22moption takes an argument that sets the name of the archive to operate upon.  The[m
       following command, written in the traditional style, instructs tar to store all files from the directory [1m/etc [22minto the archive file [1metc.tar[22m, verbosely listing the files being archived:[m
[m
       tar cfv etc.tar /etc[m
[m
       In [1mUNIX [22mor [1mshort-option style[22m, each option letter is prefixed with a single dash, as in other command line utilities.  If an option takes an argument, the argument follows it, either as a  sepa‐[m
       rate command line word, or immediately following the option.  However, if the option takes an [1moptional [22margument, the argument must follow the option letter without any intervening whitespace, as[m
       in [1m-g/tmp/snar.db[22m.[m
